    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS). \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented email-uniqueness rejection for owner creation, following the existing telephone-uniqueness pattern in the codebase.\n\n**New files:**\n- `CheckOwnerEmailUnique.java` \u2014 a function step that reads the normalized (lower-cased) email published on the request body by `ValidateOwnerFields`, and throws `OwnerEmailConflictException` if any existing owner's lower-cased email matches. An absent email never conflicts.\n- `OwnerEmailConflictException.java` \u2014 checked exception (extends `Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause.\n- `OwnerEmailConflictExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 escalation handler responding **409** with `{\"errors\": [\"email\"]}`.\n- `officefloor/escalation/...OwnerEmailConflictException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Wiring:**\n- Added a `checkEmailUnique` step to `owners.POST.yml`, inserted after `checkTelephoneUnique` (both under `govern: [ transaction ]`), before `checkHouseholdUnique`.\n\nThe comparison is case-insensitive: the incoming email is already trimmed and lower-cased during validation, and I lower-case each existing owner's email before comparing, so any casing that collides on its lower-cased form is rejected \u2014 exactly as the spec requires. The new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` passes and no existing tests regressed.",
